# $project

One [AgentFrame](https://github.com/malcmiller/agentframe) agent with one
skill, `answer`, served over A2A. Created by `agentframe new agent`; the code
is yours to edit.

| Path | Contents |
| --- | --- |
| `$package/app.py` | The `$class_name` agent and `create_app()`, which serves it. |
| `tests/test_app.py` | Tests with a scripted model, so they never call a provider. |

## Set up and check

```shell
uv sync
uv run ruff format --check
uv run ruff check
uv run mypy
uv run pytest
```

## Run

`create_app()` chooses the model from environment variables with
`model_from_env()`, so switching providers changes only configuration. With
local Llama through Ollama (the model tag must already be installed):

```shell
${env_prefix}_API_KEY=dev-only-key AGENTFRAME_MODEL_PROVIDER=ollama \
    AGENTFRAME_OLLAMA_MODEL=llama3.2:3b \
    uv run agentframe run $package.app:create_app
```

The Agent Card is at `http://127.0.0.1:8000/.well-known/agent-card.json` and
the A2A endpoint at `/a2a`. Callers send the key in the `X-API-Key` header.
Set `${env_prefix}_PUBLIC_URL` to the address callers use, if it is not
`http://127.0.0.1:8000`.

For a Microsoft Foundry deployment instead, add the `foundry` extra to
`pyproject.toml`, sign in with `az login` and set:

```shell
AGENTFRAME_MODEL_PROVIDER=foundry
AGENTFRAME_FOUNDRY_ENDPOINT=https://<resource>.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/
AGENTFRAME_FOUNDRY_DEPLOYMENT=<deployment>
AGENTFRAME_MODEL_CONTEXT_WINDOW=128000
```

## Before production

- The API key is a **development placeholder**. Use real authentication, such
  as Microsoft Entra ID: `HostConfig(auth=EntraIDAuth(tenant_id=...,
  audience=...))` with the `entra` extra.
- Review the agent's `limits` and `model_settings`, which bound every run.
- Keep secrets in environment variables or a secret store, never in code.
